## 5. `ArgumentCaptor` — Capturing and Asserting Arguments

Use `ArgumentCaptor` when you need to assert the **exact state of the object** passed to a mock — especially useful when the object is built internally by the service.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class LeadServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private LeadRepository leadRepository;

    @Captor                                              // shorthand field declaration
    private ArgumentCaptor<Lead> leadCaptor;

    @InjectMocks
    private LeadService leadService;

    @Test
    void shouldPersistLeadWithCorrectStatus() {
        // Act
        leadService.createLead("Solar Panel Installation", "John Doe");

        // Capture the argument passed to repository.save(...)
        verify(leadRepository).save(leadCaptor.capture());

        Lead capturedLead = leadCaptor.getValue();
        assertThat(capturedLead.getTitle()).isEqualTo("Solar Panel Installation");
        assertThat(capturedLead.getAssignee()).isEqualTo("John Doe");
        assertThat(capturedLead.getStatus()).isEqualTo("OPEN");   // verify internal logic set it
    }

    @Test
    void shouldCaptureMultipleSaveCalls() {
        leadService.bulkCreate(List.of("Lead A", "Lead B", "Lead C"));

        verify(leadRepository, times(3)).save(leadCaptor.capture());

        List<Lead> allCaptured = leadCaptor.getAllValues();
        assertThat(allCaptured).hasSize(3);
        assertThat(allCaptured).extracting(Lead::getTitle)
            .containsExactly("Lead A", "Lead B", "Lead C");
    }
}
```

**Rule of thumb:** Use `ArgumentCaptor` for verification only — not for stubbing. For stubbing use `any()` / `eq()` argument matchers.

