# Spring Boot Test 2.7.x — Test Slices & Integration Patterns

## 1. Test Slice Philosophy

Spring Boot test slices load **only a targeted portion** of the application context, making them much faster than `@SpringBootTest`. Each slice auto-configures only what is relevant for that layer.

| Annotation | Loads | Use For |
|---|---|---|
| `@WebMvcTest` | MVC layer only (controllers, filters, `MockMvc`) | Controller unit tests |
| `@DataMongoTest` | MongoDB layer only (repositories, `MongoTemplate`) | Repository tests |
| `@DataJpaTest` | JPA layer only (repositories, `TestEntityManager`) | JPA repository tests |
| `@SpringBootTest` | Full application context | True integration tests |

---

## 2. `@WebMvcTest` — Controller Layer Testing

`@WebMvcTest` does NOT load `@Service` or `@Repository` beans. You must provide mocks for all service dependencies using `@MockBean`.

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.web.servlet.WebMvcTest;
import org.springframework.boot.test.mock.mockito.MockBean;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.test.web.servlet.MockMvc;

import static org.mockito.ArgumentMatchers.any;
import static org.mockito.BDDMockito.given;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.request.MockMvcRequestBuilders.*;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.*;

@WebMvcTest(LeadController.class)        // only loads this controller's slice
class LeadControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;             // auto-configured, no server needed

    @MockBean
    private LeadService leadService;     // replaces real bean in Spring context with a Mockito mock

    @Test
    void shouldReturnLeadById() throws Exception {
        Lead mockLead = new Lead("L001", "Solar", "OPEN");
        given(leadService.getLeadById("L001")).willReturn(mockLead);  // BDD-style stubbing

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/leads/L001")
                .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value("L001"))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.status").value("OPEN"));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldCreateLead() throws Exception {
        String requestBody = """
            { "title": "Solar Panel", "assignee": "John" }
            """;
        Lead created = new Lead("L002", "Solar Panel", "NEW");
        given(leadService.createLead(any())).willReturn(created);

        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/leads")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content(requestBody))
            .andExpect(status().isCreated())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value("L002"));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldReturn404WhenLeadNotFound() throws Exception {
        given(leadService.getLeadById("MISSING"))
            .willThrow(new LeadNotFoundException("MISSING"));

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/leads/MISSING"))
            .andExpect(status().isNotFound());
    }

    @Test
    void shouldReturn400ForInvalidInput() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/leads")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{}"))                               // missing required fields
            .andExpect(status().isBadRequest());
    }
}
```

**Key points for `@WebMvcTest`:**
- `@MockBean` — registers a Mockito mock as a Spring bean in the slice context (different from `@Mock`).
- Use `given(...).willReturn(...)` (BDDMockito) or `when(...).thenReturn(...)` — both work identically.
- `MockMvc` performs real HTTP request/response processing (routing, serialization, filters) without a running server.
- Specify the controller class in `@WebMvcTest(MyController.class)` to limit the context to just that controller.

